  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nChilly high pressure continues to build into the area early this \nmorning, which is currently centered in ND as of 07z. Meanwhile at \nthe mid-levels, we are within zonal flow aloft with a low-amplitude \nridge working through the central Rockies just ahead of the next \nshortwave trough over the Great Basin. As the sfc high builds, this \nwill favor lows dropping into the low to mid 20s by around sunrise. \nDespite mostly clear skies and light or calm winds at the surface, \nTd depressions appear to be just large enough with enough elevated \nwinds to keep fog development at bay for the most part, aside from \nvery isolated locations. \n\nThe sfc ridge is slow to move south and east, keeping a similar air \nmass in place through the Thanksgiving holiday. Temperatures more \ntypical of mid to late December will last until then with highs in \nthe 40s and lows in the 20s. Tuesday still looks like the chilliest \nday of the week as some places may not even reach 40 degrees. A \ncouple of systems look to pass through the area during this time \nframe, the first being the aforementioned shortwave swinging across \nthe Southern Plains and the other being another shortwave coming \nfrom the Northern Plains into the MO River Valley on Wednesday. Both \nsystems look starved for moisture with plenty of dry air still in \nthe column based on forecast soundings. The second wave passes east \nof the area by Thanksgiving and phases with the first in the \nsoutheastern US.\n\nThere appears to be better agreement in the pattern Friday into the \nweekend compared to 24 hours ago, with northwest flow aloft \npersisting and energy from passing waves staying far enough away to \nkeep a dry forecast going. The main area of uncertainty is with how \namplified the trough-ridge pattern may be due to varying strength of \nthe ridge to our west in ensemble clusters. Regardless, a warming \ntrend looks to take shape with temperatures returning to the 50s \nFriday and especially the weekend, which is closer to average for \nlate November.",
